Register GDAL's drivers in the extensions that resolve driver names, so
pyogrio can open a dataset on iOS.

Same defect as `recipes/rasterio/patches/ios-driver-registry.patch`, same
cause: `flet-libgdal` ships only `libgdal.a` on iOS, so each extension links
its own copy of GDAL and each copy carries its own driver registry.

pyogrio registers in `_ogr` (`_register_drivers()` calls `GDALAllRegister()`),
but `_io` and `_geometry` resolve driver names in their own registries, which
nothing populates. Call sites on iOS arm64, counted with `otool -tV` because
every extension *defines* the symbols and `nm` therefore cannot tell you who
calls them:

    _ogr        GDALAllRegister x1, GDALGetDriverByName x57
    _io         GDALGetDriverByName x42, and no registration at all
    _geometry   GDALGetDriverByName x41, and no registration at all

This also explains why the recipe's tests pass on iOS while the package does
not work there: they exercise `_ogr`, which self-registers, and never reach
`_io`, which is where a read or a write actually happens.

`GDALAllRegister()` is idempotent, so on Android — one shared `libgdal.so`,
one registry — this changes nothing.

The same limit applies as for rasterio: the copies remain separate GDAL
instances, so configuration set through one extension still does not reach
another. This makes the package usable rather than correct in every respect;
a shared libgdal for iOS is the complete fix and would repair rasterio, fiona
and pyogrio together.

